.TH MAIL 1 "September 2026" "Petit-Ami" "Petit-Ami programs"
.SH NAME
mail, mailc \- read, keep and send mail, on the desktop or in a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mail
.RB [ \-\-fetch | \-f ]
.RB [ \-\-diag | \-d ]
.RB [ \-\-limit= \fIn\fR ]
.br
.B mailc
.RB [ \-\-fetch | \-f ]
.RB [ \-\-diag | \-d ]
.RB [ \-\-limit= \fIn\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mail
is a mail reader that keeps your mail on this machine rather than on a
server. It reads mail from IMAP servers into mbox files under
.IR ~/.amimail ,
and everything it shows is found by reading those files again.
.B mailc
is the same program built for a terminal.
.PP
The rule it is built to: the server folders are watched, not managed.
They are read and mirrored here; nothing is created, moved, renamed or
flagged on any server. The folders you make are local, live here, and
the servers are never told of them. The one change to a server the
program will ever make is taking mail off it, and that is not written
yet.
.PP
The window has the folders down the left, one section for each
account's server folders and one for the local folders, and the
messages of the folder picked on the right: who from, what kind of mail,
the subject and as much of the message as fits, and when it came. A
message opens in a window of its own, with Reply, Reply All, Forward and
Print on its menu. The strip at the foot says what is being done and how
far it has got; fetching is on a thread of its own, so the window
answers while mail arrives.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-fetch ", " \-f
Fetch as soon as the window is up, instead of waiting for Get Mail or the
timer.
.TP
.BR \-\-diag ", " \-d
Say on standard error what is being done and how long each step takes.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit= n
How many messages to take from each folder when an account has not
said, 200 to begin with; the server form sets it for each account.
.SH THE KEYBOARD
The folders and the list each mark their pick: green where the keyboard
is, cyan at the other. The program comes up with the keyboard at the
list, on the first message of the first folder.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the pick of the pane the keyboard is at. At the folders, that
shows the folder.
.TP
.B Return
At the list, opens the message picked.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Take the keyboard to the folders or to the list.
.TP
.B Alt\-F12
Put the keyboard at the menu bar. On the desktop, Alt pressed and let go
by itself does the same. Left and Right then walk the bar, Down or
Return opens an entry's list, Up and Down walk it, Return chooses.
.TP
.B Escape
Leaves a menu however it was opened, and closes the search form, the
server form, the options box, the help window, a message's menu, and a
message being read.
.TP
.B Page Up, Page Down
Move the list a page; the wheel moves it a message a notch.
.TP
.B /
In the terminal, opens the search form.
.PP
A click on a folder or a message picks it and takes the keyboard to
that pane. A double click, or Return, opens a message.
.SH THE MENUS
.TP
.B Compose
A window with the three fields a message needs and a space to write in;
Send sends it, Cancel throws it away. Everything sent goes into a local
folder called Sent.
.TP
.B Get Mail
Ask each server what folders it has and fetch what is new. The program
also looks by itself every fifteen seconds unless the server form says
otherwise; set it to zero and it fetches only when asked.
.TP
.B Search
A form modelled on the one behind Gmail's search box: who from, who to,
the subject, words it has and words it must not, size, date and which
folder. Terms match whole; with Allow wildcards on, which it is to begin
with, * stands for any run of characters and ? for one. What is found is
listed under the form with the folder each message is in, and has the
same second\-button menu a message in the list has.
.TP
.B Config
Servers..., the accounts: where mail is read from, who to log in as, how
many messages of each folder to take, and which account sends.
Options..., a box of check boxes kept with the accounts; the first is
threaded mode, on to begin with, which gathers the list into
conversations.
.TP
.B Help
The help, the text of
.IR mail.md .
.SH THE SECOND MOUSE BUTTON
On a message, a menu: Read opens it; the rest move mail into local
folders and only local ones. Move to local Trash takes the message; a
local folder for the sender's domain takes everything from that place;
one for the sender's name takes everything under that display name; one
for this thread takes the conversation; and one for each address on the
To line takes everything that went there. Each entry says how many
messages it would take. Escape, or a click anywhere else, puts the menu
away.
.SH PRINTING
Print, on the menu of a message being read, hands the message as shown,
the header lines and the text, to
.BR lp (1),
so it goes wherever lp is set to send things. The strip says it has
gone, or that lp would not take it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.amimail/account
The accounts: servers, names, passwords and counts, and the options.
Written so that only its owner can read it.
.TP
.I ~/.amimail/NAME/FOLDER.mbox
One directory for each account, named for it, and one called
.I local
for the folders that are yours. Each mailbox is mbox: the message
exactly as it arrived, headers and all, which any mail program can
read.
.TP
.IR FOLDER.mbox.idx ", " FOLDER.mbox.state
Beside each mailbox: the index the list is drawn from and the store is
searched by, a line for every message; and how far the folder has been
read from its server, so that fetching again takes only what is new.
Both are made again when they are missing or stale.
.TP
.IR mail.md ", " mail.cat ", " mail.bmp
The help, the rules that say what kind of mail a message is, and the
banner's picture, kept beside the program: looked for where the program
is, then in
.I graph_programs
beside it, then in the current directory.
.TP
.IR mail.desktop ", " mail.png
A launcher entry for the desktop and its icon. Copy the entry to
.I ~/.local/share/applications
and the picture to
.IR ~/.local/share/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/ami\-mail.png .
.TP
.IR client_tls_cert.pem ", " client_tls_key.pem
The client certificate the network library presents on a secure
connection, looked for beside the program, then in the home directory,
then in the current directory.
.SH NOTES
For Gmail the password is an application password, issued per program to
an account with two step verification on, from
myaccount.google.com/apppasswords; it is sixteen characters, and the
spaces it is shown with are not part of it. The mail server is
imap.gmail.com on port 993 and the sending server smtp.gmail.com on port
465.
.PP
Every message carries the SHA\-256 of what it is, in the index. That is
how the program knows a message it already has, whichever folder or
server it arrives from again, so nothing is stored twice.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lp (1),
.BR mbox (5)
.SH BUGS
Deleting from a server is not written. Mail sent from here goes over one
account, the one the server form marks as sending.
